Causes, each named from this run's own record. All three are HARNESS defects, and each told the model something that
was false for that turn.
  1  The first decision, fatal. The operator's typed /start arrived as a command message carrying the whole skill
     (65,686 characters). luna's first act was to call Skill(start) itself: the redundant call a small model makes
     right after a command lands. ADR-0109's user-only refusal stood IN FRONT of ADR-0063's already-loaded answer,
     so instead of "you already have it, carry on" the model was told that the skill cannot be run from here and to
     tell the operator so without retrying. The operator HAD run it, and its instructions were in the model's
     context. luna obeyed the sentence.
  2  The amplifier. luna's reply was a single line that opened with the command in backticks and went on to say the
     operator must run it. _slash_invocation read that line as the model invoking the skill by typing it, and routed
     it as Skill(start) with the rest of the sentence as its arguments (114 to 124 characters, a backtick in each).
     This happened 14 times in a row: 14 of the run's 19 tool notes are calls the model never made.
  3  The parity gap. Each routed call was stopped by the framework's Skill gate, which exits 2 with its reason on
     stderr. That is Claude Code's hook contract: on exit 2 the model reads stderr. Zak-Code read stdout only, so 14
     times the model was handed the harness default ("blocked by hook") and never the gate's own sentence, which
     says in effect that the skill is already loaded and is to be followed, not invoked again. That sentence is the
     one that points back to work.
  After the 14th block the stuck ladder's first rung fired. luna listed a directory, read a file, updated its plan
  and ended the turn with a 306-character answer telling the operator that the command is theirs to run. The turn
  ended "completed" at t+42 s and nothing followed. In sample 2 every stalled turn was followed by a new turn 600 s
  later (19:33:04 -> 19:43:04, 19:50:51 -> 20:00:52): a wake-up armed by a turn-end hook, 14 such notes across its
  turns. Here agent-state stayed IDLE and the trace holds 0 wake-up notes.
  Sample 2's opening, same seed and ceremony: update_plan, then seven shell calls, RUNNING inside 60 s. What differs
  between the two is luna's first decision. N=1 each: this pair cannot say how often either opening occurs.

